&gt; `final` doesn't buy you as much in Java as `const` does in C, or (the lack of) `mut` in <em>Rust</em>.<p>Quite true.<p>&gt; `final` only helps you avoid dumb reassignment bugs that tests should probably catch anyway ...<p>There is another reason for declaring parameters `final` in Java.  It is the only way[1] which they can be used in an anonymous class definition such as:<p><pre><code>  return new SomeClass () {
    override int addOne () {
      return aNamedOuterMethodParam + 1;
    }
  }
</code></pre>
1 - &quot;The only way&quot; as of Java 7.  Newer versions may have removed this restriction, I haven't bothered to check.

